#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125655} :activity/id #uuid "feaa62a5-6a44-51be-9efd-89d2c7e4938d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048450 :topic/id #uuid "a059bba1-760f-57e5-af3d-31e6c7347897" :topic/math-academy-id 1947} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125655 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125892 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find the **global maximum** and **global minimum** of a function $f(x,y).$ Recall that the global maximum and minimum of a function are collectively known as **global extrema.** \n\nThe procedure we will describe for finding the global extrema is based on a general principle called the **extreme-value theorem,** which states the following:\n\n*Every function that is continuous on a closed, bounded region $D$ has global extrema (i.e., both a global maximum and a global minimum) on $D$*.\n\nLet's consider the function $f(x,y) =  e^{x^2- y^2}$ on the closed  region $D=\\{(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2: \\,  x^2 +y^2  \\leq 1\\}.$ How do we find the global extrema?\n\nTo find the global extrema of a differentiable function on a closed, bounded region $D,$ we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1**:  Determine the critical points of $f$ in the interior of $D.$\n\n![](images/50/5057a4a96daa4819a70e733519c89802c28bde184183db2b4efbe69d3db36e56.png)\n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve $\\boldsymbol f' = \\mathbf 0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n![](images/b4/b48de1dcd3a240a1f1904dd290ef0665fc603c4cc5f99915915c6a12f79d74d9.svg)\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2x{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=0 \\\\ -2y{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0).$ Since the point $(0,0)$ is in the interior of $D,$ we include it in the list of candidates for extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of ${D}$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle $x^2 + y^2 =1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\langle \\cos{t}, \\sin{t} \\rangle, \\qquad t \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & ={e}^{{cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t \\in (0,2\\pi),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ({e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)})^{′} & =0 \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t){e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has three solutions, namely \n\n$$\nt =\\dfrac\\pi 2, \\: \\pi, \\: \\dfrac{3\\pi}2 \\in (0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval, namely, $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0, \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\:  \\pi, \\: \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ for each value of $t$, we obtain the following candidate points:\n\n$$\n(1,0), \\: (0,1), \\: (-1,0), \\: (0,-1)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nEvaluating $f$ at the five points we've found gives the following table of values:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(1,0)$ | $(0,1)$ | $(-1,0)$ | $(0,-1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $e$ | $1/e$ | $e$ | $1/e$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $\\dfrac1e$ at the points $(0,1), \\, (0,-1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $e$ at the points $(1,0), \\, (-1,0).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b8b379fb-f7e4-5cdb-b25a-69a6c2ae9cf2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7879 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "85def1da-b072-51a0-ae08-591a0d352331" :step/math-academy-id 45426 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125656}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125656 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124656 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124219 :question/id #uuid "14c9cf83-8dce-598b-b9cb-ec22966d8fac" :question/math-academy-id "e-12587"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e38cefde-a663-56de-a81d-dcea6fed89b3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048621}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125063} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284576} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446480} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446484} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446490}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Global Maxima and Minima Given the Critical Points Inside the Function's Domain"} :step/id #uuid "ac225680-0726-58b8-b03b-f62ad289bb51" :step/math-academy-id 45427 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125657}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125657 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124657 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124220 :question/id #uuid "84d1abb1-8b2b-5180-8924-825386924270" :question/math-academy-id "e-12588"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "608bbe95-edf0-54a6-bd05-5578ba073319" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049672} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050722}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354504} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363541} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401579} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446482} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446487} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446489}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Global Maxima and Minima of a Function Whose Domain Is a Smooth Curve"} :step/id #uuid "7c1f6a3a-a0f9-5d34-85e4-3cbe4e6bca0e" :step/math-academy-id 45430 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125658}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125658 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125893 :tutorial/content "Consider a function $f(\\mathbf{x})$ and a closed and bounded region $D$ enclosed by several curves, like the one shown below.\n\n![](images/04/042e4b736bdaa93033af34cc4ff738d1976a6595a448c8b697e717a74d19b67f.png)\n\nIn such cases, we need to search for extrema candidates among the following three categories:\n\nI. The critical points of $f$ that belong to the interior of $D.$\n\nII. The critical points of the function $f(\\mathbf{r}),$ where $\\mathbf{r}$ traverses each of the given parts of the boundary. In the example above, we would have to parametrize each of the $\\color{blue}4$ parts of the boundary separately.\n\nIII. The corner points of the boundary, i.e., the points where the different parts of the boundary meet. In the example above, we'd have to consider $\\color{red}4$ such points." :tutorial/id #uuid "e1ab24b0-a5c2-5d72-bc3e-614864010365" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7880 :tutorial/title "Determining the Candidates for Extrema on a Piecewise Smooth Boundary"} :step/id #uuid "8d1553dd-1fe4-5a34-9376-7547f1b2b500" :step/math-academy-id 45433 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125659}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125659 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124658 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124221 :question/id #uuid "138b3374-23fe-5705-87ed-e9e27a19e186" :question/math-academy-id "e-12589"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82691436-7076-5c46-bb45-462ea9218db8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049508}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125037} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284579} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351513} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446481} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446483} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446488}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Global Maxima and Minima of a Function Whose Domain Is Piecewise Smooth"} :step/id #uuid "ccc80dce-cc5f-5576-95e2-9236c9281336" :step/math-academy-id 45434}] :activity/title "Calculating Global Extrema of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124737}]}
